{"version" 2 "boxes" [{"id" "surrogate-wall" "properties" {"children" #{} "parents" #{#afield/box "root"} "name" "The wall \u2014 a threshold's exact gradient is exactly zero" "frame" #afield/rect [60.0 60.0 700.0 980.0] "code" #afield/file-inline {"path" "surrogate-wall_code_.kt" "inline" "// A `threshold` is a WALL. It lowers to a hard `step`, whose derivative is exactly zero everywhere\n// except on a measure-zero set, so a fit standing at the seed reads no signal at all and does not\n// move. This box shows the wall, and shows the object that replaces it.\n//\n// `AdSurrogate` does not smooth the shader. Every value it pushes is still the value the `Float`\n// path computes; only the local partial changes. So the loss printed here is the TRUE loss at every\n// temperature, and it is only the direction that is a surrogate.\n\nimport afield.stacksl.cpu.AdSurrogate\nimport afield.stacksl.cpu.StackSLCpu\nimport afield.stacksl.cpu.StackSLImageLoss\nimport afield.stacksl.cpu.StackSLWires\nimport afield.util.PngEncoder\n\nrun {\n\n    val seedProgram = \"uv x 0.35 threshold\"\n    val targetProgram = \"uv x 0.57 threshold\"\n    val template = StackSLCpu.stillTemplate()\n    val dim = 32\n\n    val wired = StackSLWires.lift(seedProgram)\n    val wire = wired.literalWires.first { it.token == \"0.35\" }\n    println(\"seed   : $seedProgram\")\n    println(\"target : $targetProgram      (the same program at a different edge)\")\n    println(wired.describe())\n    println()\n\n    val target = StackSLCpu.renderProgram(targetProgram, template, dim, dim)\n    val loss = StackSLImageLoss(wired, template, dim, target)\n    val seed = wired.initial\n\n    // The wall itself. Not \"small\" \u2014 exactly zero, printed as a raw bit pattern so there is no\n    // chance of reading 1e-18 as 0.\n    val exact = loss.valueAndGradient(seed).gradient[wire.index]\n    println(\"loss at the seed        : %.6e\".format(loss.value(seed)))\n    println(\"EXACT gradient of wire #${wire.index} ('${wire.token}') : $exact\")\n    println(\"  ... and is it exactly zero? \" + (exact == 0.0))\n    println()\n\n    // The surrogate. `K_t(u) = sech^2(u / 2t) / 4t` is a mollified delta: the distributional\n    // derivative of `step` is one delta at the edge, and this is that delta convolved with a bump\n    // of width t. It integrates to `tanh(1/4t)` over a unit period, so a hot temperature carries\n    // only part of the jump it stands for \u2014 which is the first half of the surrogate's bias.\n    //\n    // The honest oracle for a width-t derivative is the width-t CENTRAL DIFFERENCE of the true\n    // loss, not its derivative: as h shrinks the truth goes to zero, which is the whole problem.\n    println(\"temperature   unit-period mass   surrogate gradient   central difference at h=t   ratio\")\n    listOf(0.25, 0.1, 0.04, 0.016).forEach { t ->\n        val surrogate = AdSurrogate.stepOnly(t)\n        val g = loss.valueAndGradient(seed, surrogate).gradient[wire.index]\n        val up = seed.copyOf().also { it[wire.index] += t }\n        val down = seed.copyOf().also { it[wire.index] -= t }\n        val fd = (loss.value(up) - loss.value(down)) / (2.0 * t)\n        println(\n            \"   %.3f            %.4f          % .5e              % .5e         %s\".format(\n                t, surrogate.unitPeriodMass, g, fd,\n                if (fd == 0.0) \"(no oracle)\" else \"%.3f\".format(g / fd)\n            )\n        )\n    }\n    println()\n    println(\"The gradient is not an approximation of zero. It is the derivative of the loss AT A\")\n    println(\"SCALE - which is the only thing a descent through a jump could possibly use.\")\n\n    // And the two images, so the thing being recovered is visible rather than described. Left is\n    // the target's edge at 0.57, right is the seed's at 0.35.\n    val seedRaster = loss.render(seed)\n    val scale = 5\n    val panel = dim * scale\n    val gutter = 8\n    val width = 2 * panel + gutter\n    // A NEUTRAL grey canvas, not the near-black the other demos use: these panels are thresholded\n    // to pure black and white, and a dark gutter is invisible against them \u2014 the strip then reads\n    // as one image with four bands rather than as two panels. Looked at, not assumed.\n    val strip = IntArray(width * panel) { 0xFF7A7F87.toInt() }\n    listOf(target, seedRaster).forEachIndexed { index, raster ->\n        val argb = raster.toArgb()\n        val x0 = index * (panel + gutter)\n        for (y in 0 until panel) for (x in 0 until panel) {\n            strip[y * width + x0 + x] = argb[(y / scale) * dim + (x / scale)]\n        }\n    }\n    println()\n    println(\"panels: TARGET (edge at 0.57) | SEED (edge at 0.35)\")\n    display(PngEncoder.encode(strip, width, panel))\n\n    // Try it: change `0.35` to `0.55` and run again. The exact gradient stays exactly zero \u2014 a wall\n    // does not get shorter as you approach it \u2014 while the loss falls and the surrogate's number\n    // grows. That asymmetry is the whole reason the surrogate exists.\n}\n"} "nested" #{} "preamble" "" "autorun" false}} {"id" "surrogate-recover" "properties" {"children" #{} "parents" #{#afield/box "root"} "name" "Recovery \u2014 the same fit with the surrogate off, then on" "frame" #afield/rect [820.0 60.0 700.0 980.0] "code" #afield/file-inline {"path" "surrogate-recover_code_.kt" "inline" "// The counterfactual, run twice: the same fit, the same seed, the same target, the same sixty Adam\n// steps, with the surrogate OFF and then ON.\n//\n// This is PARAMETER RECOVERY, not fitting. The target is rendered by the same program at a known\n// edge, so \"did it work\" has an arithmetic answer and not an aesthetic one. Fitting a picture no\n// StackSL program generated is a different claim and is not made here.\n//\n// With the surrogate off the fitted value is bit-identical to the seed \u2014 an exact gradient of zero\n// is a zero step in ADAM too. That is what a wall looks like from inside an optimiser.\n\nimport afield.stacksl.cpu.AdAnnealing\nimport afield.stacksl.cpu.StackSLCpu\nimport afield.stacksl.cpu.StackSLFit\nimport afield.util.PngEncoder\nimport kotlin.math.abs\n\nrun {\n\n    val seedProgram = \"uv x 0.35 threshold\"\n    val targetProgram = \"uv x 0.57 threshold\"\n    val truth = 0.57\n    val template = StackSLCpu.stillTemplate()\n    val dim = 32\n    val iterations = 60\n\n    val target = StackSLCpu.renderProgram(targetProgram, template, dim, dim)\n\n    val started = System.nanoTime()\n    val off = StackSLFit.fit(\n        seedProgram, target, template, dim = dim, iterations = iterations, learningRate = 0.05\n    )\n    // Geometric from a quarter of a unit cell down to a percent of one, stepped once per loss\n    // evaluation. STEP only: the staircase family would also soften a noise lattice, where zero is\n    // the CORRECT derivative \u2014 see the third box for what that costs.\n    val on = StackSLFit.fit(\n        seedProgram, target, template, dim = dim, iterations = iterations, learningRate = 0.05,\n        annealing = AdAnnealing.stepOnly(iterations)\n    )\n    val seconds = (System.nanoTime() - started) / 1e9\n\n    val wire = on.fittedIndices.first()\n    val seedValue = on.seedWires[wire]\n    val offValue = off.fittedWires[wire]\n    val onValue = on.fittedWires[wire]\n    val offError = abs(offValue - truth) / truth\n    val onError = abs(onValue - truth) / truth\n\n    println(\"recovering the edge of '$seedProgram', truth $truth\")\n    println(\"$iterations Adam steps at lr=0.05, ${dim}x$dim, both arms, %.1fs total\".format(seconds))\n    println()\n    println(\"             fitted edge   relative error   loss\")\n    println(\"  seed         %.5f       %.4f          %.4e\".format(seedValue, abs(seedValue - truth) / truth, off.seedLoss))\n    println(\"  surrogate off %.5f      %.4f          %.4e\".format(offValue, offError, off.fittedLoss))\n    println(\"  surrogate on  %.5f      %.4f          %.4e\".format(onValue, onError, on.fittedLoss))\n    println()\n    println(\"  did the OFF arm move at all? \" + (offValue != seedValue))\n    println(\"  recovery improvement: %.1fx\".format(if (onError == 0.0) Double.POSITIVE_INFINITY else offError / onError))\n    println(\"  annealing: ${on.annealing}, stepped ${on.annealing?.used} times\")\n    println()\n    println(\"the re-emitted program, which is a program:\")\n    println(\"  \" + on.fittedProgram.lines().joinToString(\"\\n  \"))\n\n    // Four panels, so the number above is checkable by eye: the target's edge, the seed's edge,\n    // where the exact fit left it (nowhere), and where the surrogate fit put it.\n    val seedRaster = on.loss.render(on.seedWires)\n    val offRaster = off.loss.render(off.fittedWires)\n    val onRaster = on.loss.render(on.fittedWires)\n    val scale = 4\n    val panel = dim * scale\n    val gutter = 8\n    val width = 4 * panel + 3 * gutter\n    // A NEUTRAL grey canvas: these panels are thresholded to pure black and white, so a dark gutter\n    // is invisible against them and the four panels read as one banded image.\n    val strip = IntArray(width * panel) { 0xFF7A7F87.toInt() }\n    listOf(target, seedRaster, offRaster, onRaster).forEachIndexed { index, raster ->\n        val argb = raster.toArgb()\n        val x0 = index * (panel + gutter)\n        for (y in 0 until panel) for (x in 0 until panel) {\n            strip[y * width + x0 + x] = argb[(y / scale) * dim + (x / scale)]\n        }\n    }\n    println()\n    println(\"panels: TARGET | SEED | surrogate OFF (identical to the seed) | surrogate ON\")\n    display(PngEncoder.encode(strip, width, panel))\n\n    // Try it: replace `threshold` with `6.0 posterize palette` and set the truth to 0.52 \u2014 a\n    // quantiser rather than a predicate, and it needs the STAIRCASE family instead \u2014 add\n    // `import afield.stacksl.cpu.AdSurrogateFamily` and pass:\n    //\n    //     annealing = AdAnnealing(0.25, 0.01, iterations, setOf(AdSurrogateFamily.STAIRCASE))\n    //\n    // Continuous parameters like this edge recover to about 2% relative. An INTEGER-valued one \u2014 a\n    // level count, a bit depth \u2014 recovers to about 20%, because the loss is itself a staircase in\n    // that parameter and a bump of width t cannot see across its shelves.\n}\n"} "nested" #{} "preamble" "" "autorun" false}} {"id" "surrogate-cost" "properties" {"children" #{} "parents" #{#afield/box "root"} "name" "The cost \u2014 why a soft floor ruins a noise frequency" "frame" #afield/rect [1580.0 60.0 700.0 980.0] "code" #afield/file-inline {"path" "surrogate-cost_code_.kt" "inline" "// Why `AdSurrogate.all(...)` is not the default, and why the brief that asked for this work had\n// the question upside down.\n//\n// `fbm`'s lattice is `floor(p)`, and it was supposed to be the wall blocking a noise fit. It is the\n// opposite. `_hash21` is `fract(p * vec2(123.34, 456.21))`, `p += dot(p, p + 45.32)`,\n// `fract(p.x * p.y)` \u2014 all differentiable, none of it bit-twiddling, and utterly chaotic. Inside a\n// value-noise cell the four corner hashes ARE constants, so zero is the TRUE derivative there, and\n// `floor` is the thing keeping that chaos out of the answer.\n//\n// So this box runs the experiment the premise implies, and watches it fail on purpose.\n\nimport afield.stacksl.cpu.AdSurrogate\nimport afield.stacksl.cpu.StackSLCpu\nimport afield.stacksl.cpu.StackSLImageLoss\nimport afield.stacksl.cpu.StackSLWires\nimport kotlin.math.abs\nimport kotlin.math.max\n\nrun {\n\n    val program = \"\"\"\n        uv 3 * fbm\n        0.5 0.5 0.5 1 vec4\n        0.5 0.5 0.5 1 vec4\n        1.0 1.0 1.0 1 vec4\n        0.0 0.33 0.67 1 vec4\n        palette\n    \"\"\".trimIndent()\n\n    val template = StackSLCpu.stillTemplate()\n    val dim = 32\n\n    val wired = StackSLWires.lift(program)\n    val wire = wired.literalWires.first { it.token == \"3\" }\n    println(\"the frequency wire: #${wire.index} '${wire.token}' at ${wire.line}:${wire.column}\")\n\n    // A fixed target, so the loss is a real objective. Any picture will do here; the question is\n    // about the GRADIENT, and the oracle is a central difference of the same loss.\n    val target = StackSLCpu.renderProgram(\"uv 5 * fbm 0.2 0.5 0.7 1 vec4 palette\", template, dim, dim)\n    val loss = StackSLImageLoss(wired, template, dim, target)\n    val values = wired.initial\n\n    val h = 1e-3 * max(1.0, abs(values[wire.index]))\n    val up = values.copyOf().also { it[wire.index] += h }\n    val down = values.copyOf().also { it[wire.index] -= h }\n    val fd = (loss.value(up) - loss.value(down)) / (2.0 * h)\n    val exact = loss.valueAndGradient(values).gradient[wire.index]\n\n    println()\n    println(\"the EXACT gradient, with floor() carrying its honest zero:\")\n    println(\"  exact              % .6e\".format(exact))\n    println(\"  central difference % .6e   (h = %.1e)\".format(fd, h))\n    println(\"  relative disagreement %.3e   <- this wire ALREADY works\".format(abs(exact - fd) / max(abs(fd), 1e-12)))\n    println()\n    println(\"now switch the STAIRCASE family on, which is what a 'smoothed floor' means here:\")\n    println(\"  temperature   staircase gradient    relative disagreement\")\n    var worst = 0.0\n    var signsSeen = 0\n    listOf(0.25, 0.1, 0.04, 0.016).forEach { t ->\n        val g = loss.valueAndGradient(values, AdSurrogate.staircaseOnly(t)).gradient[wire.index]\n        val relative = abs(g - fd) / max(abs(fd), 1e-12)\n        if (relative > worst) worst = relative\n        if (g > 0.0) signsSeen = signsSeen or 1\n        if (g < 0.0) signsSeen = signsSeen or 2\n        println(\"     %.3f        % .6e         %.3e\".format(t, g, relative))\n    }\n    println()\n    // Read off the measurement rather than remembered: the exact size depends on the target and\n    // the resolution, and a sentence with a number baked into it goes stale the first time either\n    // one moves. Five to seven orders of magnitude at this target; the jvmTest fence, on a\n    // different target, measures six to eight.\n    println(\n        \"That is %.0f orders of magnitude, and the sign %s.\".format(\n            kotlin.math.log10(worst),\n            if (signsSeen == 3) \"FLIPS across the sweep\" else \"at least stays put\"\n        )\n    )\n    println(\"It gets WORSE as the schedule cools, because a narrower kernel has a taller peak and\")\n    println(\"the hash multiplies it up. A soft floor does not help this wire - it is exactly what\")\n    println(\"the wire was being protected from.\")\n    println()\n    println(\"The rule: STEP for a predicate, STAIRCASE for a quantiser, and never STAIRCASE on a\")\n    println(\"program containing noise, fbm, noise3, fbm3, worley3 or voronoi3.\")\n\n    // And the picture the wire draws, so the thing being argued about is on screen.\n    display(StackSLCpu.renderStill(program, dim = 220).toPngBytes())\n\n    // Try it: change `AdSurrogate.staircaseOnly(t)` to `AdSurrogate.stepOnly(t)`. The numbers go\n    // straight back to the exact gradient, because this program contains no `step` at all \u2014 which\n    // is the argument for a per-family surrogate rather than one switch.\n}\n"} "nested" #{} "preamble" "" "autorun" false}} {"id" "root" "properties" {"children" #{#afield/box "surrogate-wall" #afield/box "surrogate-recover" #afield/box "surrogate-cost"} "parents" #{#afield/box "metaroot"} "name" "root" "nested" #{}}}] "persistentProperties" ["frame" "name" "code" "code_language" "contents_language" "default_property" "file_proxy_mode" "file_proxy_path" "file_proxy_original_path" "file_proxy_mime" "filename" "preamble" "introduction" "attic" "notes" "fragment" "stackSL" "msl" "vertex" "vertex_fragment" "task" "mesh" "raygen" "miss" "closesthit" "anyhit" "intersection" "callable" "fragment_s" "vertex_s" "task_s" "mesh_s" "raygen_s" "miss_s" "closesthit_s" "anyhit_s" "intersection_s" "callable_s" "children" "parents" "compute" "compute_s" "customization" "source_id" "templateId" "_tombstone" "isRemoteJSRoot" "isPythonRoot" ".*_editorState" "metadata" "nested" "nestedIn" "playlist" "pauseList" "bookmarks" "slideShow" "autorun" "layers" "layerFrames" "_controlGroupValues" "ai4_auto_name" "ai4_auto_name_signature" "fileManagerAccessHistory" "isClojureRoot" "isDatalogRoot" "isForthRoot" "isFieldMLRoot" "isMorphoRoot" "isNanoRoot" "nanoLaunchDelayMs" "nanoMemoryInit" "isKotlinJsRoot" "mainLanguage" "spreadsheet"]}
